# Line-ending policy: LF everywhere, in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# Without this file the result depends on each developer's `core.autocrlf`. On a
# Windows checkout with autocrlf=true every text file lands as CRLF, and that
# breaks things that are hard to attribute: vitest cannot even collect a `.mjs`
# with CRLF (it fails with "SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token"), and
# scripts/version-files.test.mjs compares Cargo.toml lines byte-for-byte. Those
# failures never appear on CI, which runs on ubuntu, so they read as local
# regressions when they are only line endings.
#
# `eol=lf` (not just `text=auto`) is the part that matters: `text=auto` alone
# normalises what gets committed but still hands Windows a CRLF working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets. `text=auto` already detects these by content, but declaring
# them is what guarantees no future renormalisation can rewrite bytes inside an
# icon, a font or a pdfium library.
*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.svg   text eol=lf
*.woff2 binary
*.dll   binary
*.so    binary
*.dylib binary
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.tlz   binary
*.zip   binary
